          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How Long to Charge a Power Station",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-long-does-it-take-to-charge-a-portable-power-station/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use my power station while it is charging?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most current units support pass-through charging, so you can run devices while the battery charges. The battery then fills only with the input left over after the load is covered. A few older or budget models do not support it or may cycle the battery, so check the manual if you plan to rely on it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is fast charging bad for a power station battery?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers design their fast chargers within the cells' limits, so routine fast charging is generally fine. Heat is the main factor in battery wear, and fast charging produces more of it. If you are not in a hurry, a slower charge mode is gentler and quieter, and many units let you choose it in the app."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a 300Wh power station take to charge?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "From a wall brick of around 90W to 120W, about three to four hours; from a fast-charging model, often an hour or less. A car socket takes roughly three hours, and a single 100W panel in good summer sun usually needs most of a day to fill it from empty."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I charge a power station with a regular phone charger?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many units accept USB-C power delivery input, often up to 60W or 100W, so a high-wattage USB-C laptop charger works, slowly. A standard 20W phone charger adds so little that it would take days to charge a mid-size unit. Use the AC input whenever wall power is available. It is a handy trickle top-up at a desk."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my power station charge fast to 80 percent and then slow down?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "That is the battery management system tapering the current as the cells near full, which protects them and balances the cells. It is normal and happens on every lithium battery. If you do not need the last 20 percent, unplugging at 80 percent saves a lot of time. Most apps show the current charge rate live."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I charge a power station in storage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most manufacturers recommend storing the unit partly charged, often around 50 to 80 percent, and topping it up every three to six months because of slow self-discharge. Check the level before storm season and charge to full when a severe weather warning goes out so it is ready when needed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a generator charge a portable power station?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Plug the power station's AC charging cable into the generator as you would a wall outlet. Make sure the generator can supply the charging wattage, or lower the power station's charge rate. An inverter generator provides clean power that power station chargers handle without trouble. Keep the generator outdoors, well away from windows and doors."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
