    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most modern portable power stations charge from a wall outlet in about 1 to 2 hours thanks to built-in fast charging, while older or budget units with a separate power brick can take 6 to 10 hours. From a car's 12V socket, which usually delivers only about 100W to 120W, a 1,000Wh unit takes roughly 10 to 12 hours, and solar time depends on panel wattage and sun: a 200W panel typically puts back 600Wh to 900Wh on a good summer day. The quick formula for any source is battery capacity divided by real input watts, plus about 10 to 20 percent for losses and the slower final stretch. Temperature and the battery management system's taper above roughly 80 percent explain most of the gap between the advertised time and what you actually see.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. So How Long Does a Portable Power Station Take to Charge?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It depends almost entirely on how you charge it. A power station is a battery, and charging time is simply how many watt-hours the battery holds divided by how many watts are flowing in. Wall outlets can push a lot of power, cars and small panels push very little, and that difference is why the same 1,000Wh unit can take one hour or two days to fill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wall charging is where the big change has happened. Most current models from the major brands have an internal charger that accepts AC power directly at high wattage, often 1,000W to 1,800W, and they routinely advertise 0 to 80 percent in under an hour and a full charge in one to two hours. Older units and many budget models still use an external adapter in the 90W to 200W range, and those take most of a working day.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Charge Times, 1,000Wh Unit</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1-2 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wall, built-in fast charger</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">6-10 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wall, external brick</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-12 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Car 12V socket</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1-2 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">One 200W solar panel</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Car charging is slow on every unit because the 12V accessory socket is the bottleneck, not the power station. Solar sits in between and varies most: panel wattage, season, weather, panel angle and the power station's own solar input limit all decide the result. A large array on a summer day can rival a wall charger; a single small panel in winter may barely keep up with a phone and a light.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sections below give you the formula, typical times for each source in a table, and the specific reasons a charge takes longer than the box says. If you are still deciding how big a unit to buy, keep charging in mind: a battery twice the size takes twice as long to refill from the same source, which matters most if solar or a car is your only way to top up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="formula" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Do You Calculate Charging Time?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The working formula is: charge time in hours equals battery capacity in watt-hours divided by actual input watts, then multiplied by about 1.1 to 1.2. The multiplier covers conversion losses in the charging circuitry and the slower final phase of the charge, when the battery management system eases off the current as the cells approach full. It is a planning estimate, not a lab figure, but it lands close for most units.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important word is actual. The input figure that matters is what the power station is really receiving, which is often less than the rating on the charger or panel. Most units show live input watts on their display or app, and that number is the one to use. If the screen says 92W from your car socket, a 1,000Wh battery will take around 11 or 12 hours, regardless of what the charging cable is rated for.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">100W input</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">200W input</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">500W input</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">1,200W input</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">300Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~3.5 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.8 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~45 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~20 min (if supported)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">500Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~6 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~3 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.2 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~30 min (if supported)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,000Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~11-12 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~6 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.3 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2,000Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~23 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~11-12 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4.6 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2 hrs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3,600Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~41 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~21 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~8 hrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~3.5 hrs</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two worked examples show how it plays out. A 500Wh unit with a 120W wall brick: 500 divided by 120 is about 4.2 hours, and adding 15 percent gives roughly 4.8 hours. A 2,000Wh unit on 400W of solar that is actually delivering 300W: 2,000 divided by 300 is about 6.7 hours of strong sun, plus 15 percent is nearly 8 hours, which is more strong sun than most days provide. That unit needs two good days from that array.
          </p>

          <p>
            You rarely charge from completely empty, so scale the result. Topping up from 40 percent to full on a 1,000Wh unit means putting back about 600Wh, so roughly 60 percent of the full-charge time. If your goal is enough charge rather than a full battery, stopping at 80 percent also skips the slowest part of the charge, which is covered further down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Long Does It Take to Charge From a Wall Outlet?
          </h2>

          <p>
            With a built-in fast charger, about one to two hours for most units up to roughly 2,000Wh, and two to three hours for the largest single units. These chargers draw a lot of power from the outlet, commonly 1,000W to 1,800W at full speed, which is why they are fast. Many units let you lower the charge rate in the settings or app, trading speed for quieter fans and less heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fast charging has a practical limit at the outlet. A standard US 15-amp, 120V household circuit tops out around 1,800W, and the electrical code generally treats 80 percent of that, about 1,440W, as the limit for a continuous load. A power station drawing 1,500W or more on a circuit that is also running a microwave or space heater can trip the breaker. If that happens, charge on a different circuit or turn the charge rate down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Units with an external charging brick are much slower because the brick limits the power. Adapters in the 90W to 200W range are common on older and budget models, putting a 500Wh unit at four to six hours and a 1,000Wh unit at eight hours or more. Some of these units also accept a second input, such as solar or a USB-C power delivery charger, at the same time to shorten the charge.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE GENERATOR SHORTCUT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A generator charges a power station just like a wall outlet</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Plug the power station's AC charging cable into a generator and it charges at its normal wall speed, provided the generator can supply that wattage. An inverter generator gives clean power that the charger handles easily. If the generator is small, lower the power station's charge rate so it does not overload it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Wall charging is the one to use whenever you can, including before a storm. Fully charge the unit when a severe weather warning goes out, then top it up every few months during storage. A battery that is already full when the power goes out saves you from relying on a car or panels for the first, most critical hours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="car" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Long Does It Take to Charge From a Car?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plan on a long time. A car's 12V accessory socket is usually fused at 10 to 15 amps, and most power stations limit their car-charging input to about 8 to 10 amps to stay within that, which works out to roughly 100W to 120W. At that rate, a 300Wh unit takes around three hours, a 1,000Wh unit around 10 to 12 hours, and a 2,000Wh unit a full day of driving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the engine while charging. A car's starting battery is not designed for deep discharge, and drawing 100W from it for hours with the engine off can leave you unable to start the car. Many vehicles also cut power to the accessory socket when the ignition is off. Charging while you drive, or while the engine idles in a safe, well-ventilated outdoor spot, is the safe way to do it. Never idle a vehicle in an attached garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some power stations accept higher-current 12V or 24V inputs, and several major brands sell dedicated alternator chargers, essentially DC-to-DC chargers wired directly to the vehicle's battery, that deliver several hundred watts. These turn a car or RV into a much faster charger, but they require proper installation with correctly sized wiring and fusing. They suit people who camp or work from a vehicle regularly; for occasional outage use, the accessory socket is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat the car as a backup charger rather than a primary one. A tank of gas can charge a power station repeatedly over a multi-day outage, which is genuinely useful, but the 100W ceiling means it supports low-power needs like phones, lights, a CPAP or a router far better than a refrigerator. Our guide to whether a <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">power station can run a CPAP</a> shows how far a modest daily top-up goes for small loads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Long Does It Take to Charge With Solar Panels?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solar time depends on three things: panel wattage, peak sun hours and real-world efficiency. Daily energy is roughly panel watts times peak sun hours times about 0.7 to 0.8. Panels rarely deliver their full rating outside of ideal test conditions, because heat, angle, haze, dust and wiring losses all chip away at it. On a portable setup laid against a rock or propped on a kickstand, 70 to 80 percent of the rating at midday is a typical good result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Peak sun hours are not the same as daylight hours. They measure the equivalent hours of full-strength sunshine, and across much of the US they range from about five to seven in summer and two to four in winter. Put those numbers together: a 200W panel on a summer day yields roughly 700Wh to 1,000Wh, which can nearly fill a 1,000Wh unit in one long, clear day. The same panel in December may produce 300Wh to 500Wh.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Solar array</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Summer day (~5.5 sun hrs)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Winter day (~3 sun hrs)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fills a 1,000Wh unit in</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~400Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~200Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 summer days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~800Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~450Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-1.5 summer days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">400W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1,650Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~900Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under one summer day</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">800W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~3,300Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1,800Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 hours of strong sun</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The power station's solar input limit caps everything. Many mid-size units accept 400W to 600W, and larger models 1,000W or more, often with a required voltage range. Panels beyond that limit simply do not add power. Many units can also combine AC and solar input at the same time for faster charging when both are available. For setup details, our guide on <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">charging a power station with solar</a> covers angles, wiring and input limits, and our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best portable solar panel</a> picks show which panels deliver close to their rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Positioning makes a large difference. Point the panel directly at the sun, adjust it two or three times a day as the sun moves, and keep it out of even partial shade, since shading a small part of a panel can cut its output dramatically. Keep panels cool where possible by leaving air space behind them, because output drops as panel temperature rises. Those simple steps often add more energy than buying a bigger panel would.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="slowdowns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Is My Power Station Charging Slowly?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common reason is the taper. Lithium batteries charge at full speed through most of their range, then the battery management system reduces the current as the cells approach full to balance them and avoid overcharging. On many units this slowdown starts somewhere around 80 to 90 percent. That is why the advertised 0 to 80 percent time is often much shorter than half of the full-charge time, and why the last 10 percent can take surprisingly long.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature is the next big factor. Most power station manufacturers specify a charging range of roughly 32°F to 104°F, or 0°C to 40°C, and lithium batteries can be damaged by charging below freezing. A cold unit may refuse to charge or charge very slowly until it warms up; a few newer models include battery heaters for this reason. A hot unit, such as one sitting in direct sun next to its own panels, will throttle its input to protect itself. Keep the power station in the shade with the panels in the sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running loads during charging also slows it. If the power station is charging at 300W from solar while running a 100W load, only about 200W goes into the battery. Pass-through charging is convenient, but the battery fills only with whatever input is left over. Check the display: most show both input and output watts, which tells you the net charging rate at a glance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other culprits are a charge-rate setting left on slow or quiet mode, a cable or adapter that is not the original, a car socket that drops voltage under load, and on solar, panels wired so their voltage falls outside the unit's input range. An aging battery can also charge differently as its capacity falls. If a unit that used to charge quickly now crawls with the same setup, our guide on <a href="/how-long-do-portable-power-stations-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long portable power stations last</a> explains what to look for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faster" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Can You Charge a Power Station Faster?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use the wall whenever you have it, at the highest charge rate the circuit can handle. If you know an outage or trip is coming, charging to full from the grid beforehand removes the time pressure. During an outage, combine sources where the unit allows it: many models accept AC and solar together, or solar and car input together, and the inputs add up.
          </p>

          <p>
            For solar, more panel wattage up to the unit's limit is the main lever, followed by aiming the panels and keeping them unshaded. If your unit accepts 600W of solar and you own 200W of panels, adding panels is the most direct upgrade. If you are already at the limit, a second battery or a unit with a higher solar input is the next step.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charge to 80 or 90 percent when time matters. Because the final stretch is the slowest, stopping short of full gives you most of the energy in a fraction of the time. Many apps let you set a charge limit, which also reduces long-term wear on the battery for units that spend most of their life plugged in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, manage heat. Keep the unit in the shade with its vents clear, out of a hot car, and away from heaters. A power station that is not throttling for temperature charges at its rated speed. In cold weather, bring it indoors to warm up before charging, since a battery below freezing should not be charged at all. Those habits cost nothing and recover much of the speed lost to conditions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the panel rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 200W panel rarely delivers 200W. Heat, angle, haze and wiring losses typically leave 70 to 80 percent of the rating at best, and much less in winter or partial shade. Plan solar charge times around real output, which your power station's display shows, rather than the number printed on the panel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging from the car with the engine off</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drawing 100W from a car's starting battery for hours can leave you stranded with a car that will not start. Many vehicles also cut accessory power when the ignition is off. Charge while driving or while idling outdoors in a well-ventilated spot, and never idle in an attached garage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging a frozen battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most manufacturers say not to charge below about 32°F, because charging lithium cells below freezing can damage them. A unit stored in a cold garage or car should warm up indoors before you plug it in. Some newer models have built-in heaters, but most do not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fast charging on a busy circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A power station drawing 1,500W or more on wall power can trip a 15-amp breaker shared with a microwave, kettle or heater. Charge on a circuit with little else running, or lower the charge rate in the settings so the total draw stays within what the circuit can handle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting for 100 percent when you are in a hurry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The last 10 to 20 percent is the slowest part of any lithium charge because the battery management system tapers the current. If you need to leave or the sun is setting, 80 or 90 percent arrives much sooner and covers nearly as much use.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use my power station while it is charging?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most current units support pass-through charging, so you can run devices while the battery charges. The battery then fills only with the input left over after the load is covered. A few older or budget models do not support it or may cycle the battery, so check the manual if you plan to rely on it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is fast charging bad for a power station battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers design their fast chargers within the cells' limits, so routine fast charging is generally fine. Heat is the main factor in battery wear, and fast charging produces more of it. If you are not in a hurry, a slower charge mode is gentler and quieter, and many units let you choose it in the app.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 300Wh power station take to charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                From a wall brick of around 90W to 120W, about three to four hours; from a fast-charging model, often an hour or less. A car socket takes roughly three hours, and a single 100W panel in good summer sun usually needs most of a day to fill it from empty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I charge a power station with a regular phone charger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many units accept USB-C power delivery input, often up to 60W or 100W, so a high-wattage USB-C laptop charger works, slowly. A standard 20W phone charger adds so little that it would take days to charge a mid-size unit. Use the AC input whenever wall power is available. It is a handy trickle top-up at a desk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my power station charge fast to 80 percent and then slow down?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That is the battery management system tapering the current as the cells near full, which protects them and balances the cells. It is normal and happens on every lithium battery. If you do not need the last 20 percent, unplugging at 80 percent saves a lot of time. Most apps show the current charge rate live.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I charge a power station in storage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most manufacturers recommend storing the unit partly charged, often around 50 to 80 percent, and topping it up every three to six months because of slow self-discharge. Check the level before storm season and charge to full when a severe weather warning goes out so it is ready when needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a generator charge a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Plug the power station's AC charging cable into the generator as you would a wall outlet. Make sure the generator can supply the charging wattage, or lower the power station's charge rate. An inverter generator provides clean power that power station chargers handle without trouble. Keep the generator outdoors, well away from windows and doors.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

