        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A portable power station charges in about one to two hours from the wall if it has a built-in fast charger, six to ten hours with an external brick, ten or more hours from a car socket, and anywhere from a few hours to a couple of days on solar depending on panel wattage and season. Divide the battery's capacity by the real input watts shown on the display, add 10 to 20 percent, and you have a reliable estimate for your own setup.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            To charge faster, use wall power whenever it is available, combine inputs if your unit allows it, aim panels at the sun and keep them unshaded, and stop at 80 or 90 percent when time is short. Keep the unit warm enough to charge and cool enough not to throttle, and it will charge close to its rated speed.
          </p>
